Abstract—This paper presents an energy efficient machine learning framework for the detection of Parkinson’s Disease (PD) utilizing wearable sensor data from the publicly accessible Parkinson’s Disease Smartwatch (PADS) dataset. A systematic feature engineering strategy is devised to improve the differentiation of motor symptoms by combining windowed squared energy features in the time domain with FFT based spectral band power features for tremor characterization. The study performs a systematic comparative analysis of various machine learning architectures, including Support Vector Machines (SVM), Artificial Neural Networks (ANN), Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN), Bidirectional Long Short Term Memory (BiLSTM) networks, Random Forests, and an ANN–BiLSTM ensemble. Experimental outcomes indicate that energy based feature reformulation, when integrated with spectral representation, improves classification performance by up to 16.3 percentage points compared to conventional linear energy features, with the Random Forest and ANN–BiLSTM ensemble attaining a peak accuracy of 82.86% . These results show that energy amplified feature modeling works well for wearable based PD discrimination and set up a scalable, data driven system for objective monitoring of Parkinson’s Disease. Index Terms—Parkinson’s Disease, Wearable Sensors, Feature Extraction, Machine Learning, Time-Frequency Analysis
I. INTRODUCTION
Parkinson’s Disease is the most common neurodegenerative disorder of the central nervous system characterized by the cardinal motor symptoms attributable to the loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta, which is
the pathological hallmark of the disease [1]. Motor symptoms arise because of the lack of dopamine in the basal ganglia, a set of brain structures responsible for the control of movements, and thus are accompanied by non motor complications like insomnia, dementia, and autonomic dysfunction [2]. Levodopa is still the most effective symptomatic therapy for this disease, but motor fluctuations and dyskinesias have been observed with its chronic use, thus requiring the need for advanced monitoring methods [3]. The diagnosis of PD is very challenging and depends heavily on clinical judgement that has been also confounded by co, existing conditions such as essential tremors, dystonias, multiple system atrophy, and progressive supranuclear palsy, which have similar movement symptoms but different pathological mechanisms and responses to therapy [4]. The latest technological breakthrough in wearable technology has provided the means for the continuous capturing of high, resolution movement signals in an objective manner. This creates possibilities for the use of machine learning algorithms for analysis. Machine learning algorithms could identify discriminable movement features that may also be able to distinguish PD from other movement disorders.
