Empirical Verification.
Using squared signal energy, a classification accuracy improvement of 16.3 percentage points was observed when compared to simple summation, resulting in 12 additional correctly identified cases out of 71 test samples. Confusion matrix analysis further indicated that the use of squared energy increased the Healthy class recall from 37.5% to 62.5%, demonstrating a substantial reduction in false positive predictions due to improved signal-to-noise ratio (SNR)[5]. These results reinforce prior findings that energy based representations are particularly effective for capturing tremor related motor characteristics[9], and confirm that squared signal energy serves as a robust feature for Parkinson’s Disease classification using wearable sensor data.
