IV. PROPOSED METHODOLOGY
The proposed PD detection architecture employs three axis accelerometer and gyroscope data obtained from wrist worn sensors [1]. The goal is to differentiate between Parkinson’s Disease patients and healthy controls by means of conventional as well as deep learning methods. Data preparation and feature extraction raw signals are processed through detrending method to get rid of baseline drift [17], then the signals are standardized by z scores and finally, segmented into fixed length segments. Features in the temporal and spectral domains are obtained such as FFT, bandpower and windowed statistics to measure tremor and movement variability [2], [5], [11]. The bias is reduced by excluding the error data and age variables [1], [6]. Evaluation of classifier performance: SVM, RF, ANN, CNN, and BiLSTM[2], [3], [6]. Feature based models utilize derived metrics, while BiLSTM works directly with the original time series data for extracting temporal features[4], [12]. A combination of spatial and temporal features is realized through the soft voting of ensemble ANN+BiLSTM models[4], [14], [16]. A subject wise stratified 80/20 split was used to ensure that all recordings from a given participant were assigned exclusively to either the training or testing set. This prevents subject level data leakage and ensures that the evaluation reflects the model’s ability to generalize to unseen individuals rather than memorizing subject-specific motion patterns.
A. Block Diagram
This paper presents a two path machine learning framework for Parkinson Disease (PD) detection from a special Parkinson Disease Smartwatch (PADS) dataset that comprises triaxial accelerometer and gyroscope signals recorded at 100 Hz [1]. The proposed model architecture merges feature based learning along with deep learning on raw time series data to capture different motor features indicative of Parkinsonian symptoms. The Parkinson’s Disease (PD) Smartwatch dataset (PADS) technique based data preprocessing of inertial sensors signals is carried out in the first path [1]. A low pass filtering technique based on l1 with a cutoff frequency of 50 Hz is used for the purpose of removing high frequency noise from accelerometer data [19]. The first 48 samples of each recording are removed to get rid of sensor initialization artifacts. Then, both time domain and frequency domain features are extracted. Time domain features include mean, standard deviation, root mean square (RMS), kurtosis, skewness, range, and interquartile range (IQR) while frequency domain features contain Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) coefficients, spectral entropy, dominant frequency and power spectral density (PSD) [2], [3]. These hand crafted features are used for training traditional and shallow learning algorithms such as Support Vector Machines (SVM), Artificial Neural Networks (ANN),
