V. MACHINE LEARNING MODELS
Six different machine learning and deep learning models were trained and tested for the classification of Parkinson’s Disease (PD) using wearable inertial sensor data following a unified preprocessing pipeline. This included identical signal conditioning, feature extraction, normalization, and class balancing. Such a standardized setup guarantees a fair and meaningful comparison among the models. Three of the most reliable and clinically relevant models, SVM, RF, and an ensemble framework gave the best performance in the experiment and are thus discussed in this section [2], [6], [11].
A. Random Forest
Random Forests (RF) were considered as an ensemblebased classical learning method that can capture complex feature interactions while still being interpretable [3], [6]. The model uses multiple decision trees that are trained on different
bootstrapped samples of the data and with randomly selected features to split the nodes, thus helping to reduce overfitting and increase robustness [11]. RF demonstrated a good classification accuracy at a relatively low computational cost and also provided interpretable feature importance measures, which indicated the significance of tremor-related frequency bands and movement variability [7], [6]. Such a level of interpretability makes Random Forests appealing not only for screening but also for exploratory clinical analysis [3], [7].The Random Forest classifier was implemented with 100 decision trees and a maximum tree depth of 10, using the square-root feature selection rule during node splitting.
B. Support Vector Machine (SVM)
The Support Vector Machine (SVM) method was chosen due to its excellent generalization ability when dealing with high dimensional feature spaces and its robustness even when the number of samples is small. In order to model the potentially non linear dependencies of movement features on the disease status, a Support Vector Classification (SVC) model with a Radial Basis Function (RBF) kernel was utilized. The regularization parameter was set to =10. 0, whereas the kernel width parameter was calculated on the basis of the scale heuristic [2], [3]. The SVM model was trained using a regularization parameter C = 1.0 and an RBF kernel with γ = scale, using an 80/20 stratified train–test split with random_state = 42 for reproducibility.Balanced class weights were introduced in the training phase to address the unbalanced classes of Parkinson’s Disease patients and healthy controls [10]. All input features were normalized to have unit variance before training. The SVM model proved to be consistent in its results, especially when the features based on gyroscope data were part of the input which is in line with the fact that these movements are angular velocity changes that can be linked to symptoms such as rigidity, bradykinesia, and postural instability [15]. Besides high accuracy, the model is efficient in terms of speed and computing power, which suggests possible use in situations with limited resources or a focus on screening [6].
C. Ensemble Model: Artificial Neural Networks + Bidirectional Long Short Term Memory
In order to enhance prediction robustness it was decided to come up with an ensemble framework which combines an Artificial Neural Networks (ANN) and a Bidirectional Long Short Term Memory (BiLSTM) network using weighted soft voting [4], [12]. The ANN is able to extract global statistical patterns from the manually generated features whereas, the BiLSTM captures the temporal sequential dependencies in the motor behavior [4], [12]. The ANN architecture consisted of fully connected layers (4092 → 256 → 128 → 64 → 1) with ReLU activation, batch normalization, dropout (0.3), and Adam optimization with learning rate 0.001.The BiLSTM network contained two bidirectional LSTM layers (total 256 units) followed by dense layers (256 → 64 → 1), trained using Adam optimizer (learning rate 0.001) with binary crossentropy loss and early stopping.The output class probabilities
