VI. RESULTS AND ANALYSIS
Overall evaluation results show that the Support Vector Machine (SVM), Random Forest (RF), and the ANN-BiLSTM ensemble always scored higher compared to other models in terms of accuracy, recall and F1 score metrics. The superior performance of RF and ANN-BiLSTM indicates that squaredenergy feature amplification enhances the discriminative margin between Parkinsonian and healthy.
More importantly, our proposed framework was validated using cross validation on unseen test partitions of the dataset which was not involved in any way during the training process. After following the same preprocessing and feature extraction steps, the best models still showed a similar level of performance on the new data, hence they are indicating stable generalization across validation splits. The squaredenergy formulation produced an absolute enhancement of up to 16.3 percentage points relative to linear energy features, signifying improved discriminative ability for Parkinsonian motor signals.
As shown in Table II, classification accuracies ranged from 77% to 83%, with the Random Forest and ANN-BiLSTM ensemble models achieving the highest accuracy of 82.86%. The ensemble model benefited from combining feature-based learning with temporal modeling, resulting in improved generalization capability [8]. Confusion matrix analysis demonstrated strong diagonal dominance across all evaluated models, indicating consistent classification performance across classes.
