VII. FUTURE SCOPE
This study establishes a solid basis for detecting Parkinson’s Disease (PD) through triaxial accelerometer and gyroscope data from smartwatch based wearable sensors. The system could be further enhanced by developing the model to be clinically more relevant with an emphasis on detection of pathological markers during the prodromal phase. In any case, the lack of early stage PD data with reliable clinical labels remains the biggest challenge in the identification of mild symptoms with high accuracy [1], [6]. More extensive and diverse cohorts comprising young individuals at risk and pre diagnostic populations would be beneficial in training the model for greater sensitivity to early motor symptoms, which is critical for timely clinical intervention [13].
Embedding the framework into smartwatches, phones, or fitness bands allows constant, quiet tracking of motor symptoms during daily activities [16]. The feature extraction uses simple math like windowed squared-energy and Fast Fourier Transform, which run quickly. This lets motion signals be analyzed in short time frames on small devices. The system can track tremor, walking problems, and changes in movement over time. Clinicians would get long-term data to assess condition and manage disease[17], [18]. Adding voice, sleep patterns, and fine motor actions might boost accuracy in diagnosing neurodegenerative diseases. Privacy methods like federated learning and processing data directly on the device are necessary for wide clinical use. These approaches protect user information while supporting real-world implementation [13], [16].
